workedExamples.solution_text:
Find the $y$-intercept by setting $x=0$:
$\begin{aligned}y&=|2(0)-2|\\&=2\end{aligned}$
Find the $x$-intercept:
$\begin{aligned}2x-2&=0\\2x&=2\\x&=1\end{aligned}$
The vertex is $(1,0)$ and the $y$-intercept is $(0,2)$. Draw two symmetric straight arms opening upwards.
For $x<1$, the expression $2x-2$ is negative:
$\begin{aligned}y&=-(2x-2)\\&=-2x+2\end{aligned}$
For $x\ge1$, the right arm is $y=2x-2$. The arm gradients are $-2$ and $2$, and the axis of symmetry is $x=1$.
Domain: $(-\infty,\infty)$. Range: $[0,\infty)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{mathblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->,black] (-2,0)--(4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-0.7)--(0,4.7) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,dashed] (1,0)--(1,4.3);
\draw[mathblue,thick,<->] (-1,4)--(1,0)--(3,4);
\fill[mathblue] (1,0) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[mathblue] (0,2) circle (1.5pt);
\path (1,0) node[below] {$(1,0)$} (0,2) node[left] {$(0,2)$} (1,4.3) node[above] {$x=1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

